    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ88AAAABE06tFg",
      "title": "feat(proxyd): add configurable per-method RPC cache TTLs",
      "author": "jelias2",
      "number": 748,
      "body": "**Description**\n\nProxyd currently caches a fixed list of immutable RPC methods. Add opt-in, Redis-only per-method TTLs so repeated read requests near the chain tip can reuse responses briefly:\n\n```toml\n[cache]\nenabled = true\n\n[cache.method_ttls]\neth_getBlockByNumber = \"1s\"\neth_getBalance = \"1s\"\neth_call = \"1s\"\n```\n\nEach method plus its complete original parameters has a separate cache entry; request IDs do not participate. All argument combinations qualify, including numeric blocks and `pending`. Errors, null results, and locally synthesized responses are skipped. Preserve original parameters across backend rewriting so lookup and insertion use the same key.\n\n**Tests**\n\nCache unit and HTTP integration tests pass, covering:\n- Config decoding, positive whole-second TTL validation, and rejection of known write/state-consuming methods.\n- Argument separation, caller IDs, all block selectors, expiry, skipped results, and block rewriting.\n- Legacy Redis key compatibility and unchanged default method eligibility.\n- Timed methods bypassing the cache without Redis, including overrides of built-in cached methods.\n- Redis outages: timed methods continue forwarding to backends without memory fallback, while legacy immutable methods retain their existing fallback.\n- Existing cache/read-replica integration behavior.\n\n**Additional context**\n\nExisting configs need no changes. Legacy Redis keys, immutable method defaults, global TTL default (1h), memory LRU behavior, and immutable-cache memory fallback are preserved. Redis writes and command metrics still use `SETEX`.\n\nNew method TTLs require Redis and never use memory fallback. Without Redis configured, timed methods bypass caching. Redis read/write failures use the existing fail-open cache path; existing Redis startup checks still apply. Explicit method TTLs override built-in policy for that method. Unlisted methods remain unchanged.\n\nPer-method TTLs must be positive whole seconds (`\"1s\"`, `\"5s\"`, `\"1m\"`); fractional seconds are rejected. Keys include the TTL to isolate rolling policy changes. HTTP only; concurrent misses are not coalesced. Timed entries accept added staleness and are not reorg-aware. Configure only read-only custom methods; known transaction-submission, filter creation/polling, and subscription methods are rejected.\n",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-23T21:33:16Z",
      "mergedAt": null,
      "additions": 391,
      "deletions": 21
    },
    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ88AAAABEavCrQ",
      "title": "ci: run APKO smoke tests on Docker-capable runners",
      "author": "falcorocks",
      "number": 746,
      "body": "## What\n\n- Pin all APKO factory stages to `b87283a8bb6c325da0ef18400eb819b5979a9bd3`.\n- Inherit the factory's Docker-capable smoke runners and image pull and architecture preflight.\n\n## Why\n\nThe previous factory revision defaulted amd64 smoke jobs to `ubuntu-slim`, which has the Docker client but no daemon. A non-release job could therefore pass without starting the image because Docker's exit code 1 was tolerated and no version assertion was active.\n\nThe pinned revision includes https://github.com/ethereum-optimism/factory/pull/62. It defaults amd64 smoke jobs to `ubuntu-24.04`, retains `ubuntu-24.04-arm` for arm64, pulls each image before the command loop, and verifies its architecture.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `actionlint .github/workflows/build-images.apko.yaml`\n- `git diff --check`\n- pinned planner produces 20 smoke legs on Docker-capable runners\n",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-21T12:23:31Z",
      "mergedAt": "2026-09-21T15:34:38Z",
      "additions": 4,
      "deletions": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ88AAAABEmBMIg",
      "title": "ci: use validated Melange runner profiles",
      "author": "falcorocks",
      "number": 747,
      "body": "Melange builds now use Factory’s validated compute profiles while retaining the same runners and architectures. The catalog and planner/workflow pins migrate together; publishing, smoke testing, and runner access policies retain their current configuration.\n\nValidation: old/new planner comparisons preserve resolved runner choices and existing matrix values across manual, pull-request, and non-release-tag scenarios; Actionlint passes.\n",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-22T14:27:12Z",
      "mergedAt": "2026-09-23T09:58:25Z",
      "additions": 3,
      "deletions": 7
    },
    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ88AAAABE3ghoA",
      "title": "proxyd: return HTTP 400 for oversized responses",
      "author": "bitwiseguy",
      "number": 749,
      "body": "## Summary\n\nReturn HTTP 400 when proxyd rejects an oversized backend response, while preserving the existing JSON-RPC error:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"code\": -32020,\n  \"message\": \"backend response too large\"\n}\n```\n\n## Context\n\nLarge `eth_getLogs` responses exceeding proxyd’s configured 10 MiB response limit were being returned as HTTP 500s.\n\nThese errors are generally caused by a client query producing more data than proxyd permits, rather than an unavailable or malfunctioning RPC service. Returning HTTP 400 more accurately identifies the failure as client-actionable and prevents it from contributing to server-error rates.\n\nAlthough motivated by `eth_getLogs`, this applies to any backend response exceeding `max_response_size_bytes`. `eth_getLogs` accounts for nearly all observed occurrences.\n\n## Observability\n\nThese failures will move from:\n\n```promql\nproxyd_http_response_codes_total{status_code=\"500\"}\n```\n\nto:\n\n```promql\nproxyd_http_response_codes_total{status_code=\"400\"}\n```\n\nThey will also remain visible through:\n\n```promql\nproxyd_rpc_errors_total{error_code=\"-32020\"}\n```\n\nand the `backend response too large` warning logs.",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-24T04:27:15Z",
      "mergedAt": "2026-09-24T14:15:01Z",
      "additions": 2,
      "deletions": 2
    }
  ],
